Package: oneplus-erhai-video
Version: 1.14
Section: video
Priority: optional
Architecture: arm64
Maintainer: TheMojoMan <themojoman@gmx.net>
Depends: libva2, libva-av1fix (>= 2.23.0+av1fix2)
Recommends: ffmpeg-av1fix
Description: Hardware video decoding for the OnePlus Pad 3 (erhai, SM8750)
 Userspace stack for the Qualcomm Iris hardware video decoder (the Venus
 lineage, "Iris Decoder" V4L2 node, qcom_iris kernel module) of the OnePlus
 Pad 3. Ships the iris-vaapi VA-API driver (iris_drv_video.so) so VA-API
 clients drive /dev/videoN directly, plus all browser integration:
 . - /etc/environment.d/55: LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=iris (libva cannot autodetect
   the driver on msm platform devices)
 . - /etc/environment.d/56: MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX=1 (Firefox VA-API decode
   runs in the RDD process, whose seccomp sandbox blocks /dev/dma_heap)
 . - /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json: locked VA-API decode prefs
 . - /usr/local/bin/firefox: PATH-shadowing wrapper that withholds the AV1
   entrypoint from Firefox (IRIS_VAAPI_AV1=0) while VA-API stays enabled
 . - /etc/chromium.d/erhai-hw-video: AcceleratedVideoDecoder feature flag
   (chromium's V4L2VideoDecoder drives the iris node directly, no VA-API)
 . - /etc/brave/policies/managed/erhai-hwvideo.json: HardwareAccelerationMode
 . - udev rule giving the video group access to /dev/dma_heap/system
 Codecs: H.264, HEVC Main/Main10, VP9 Profile 0/2, AV1 Main (8-bit and
 10-bit). Chromium
 (xtradeb) decodes H.264/VP9 via V4L2; Firefox and Brave decode H.264/HEVC
 Main (and Main10/VP9 P2 where the firmware exposes P010) via VA-API.
 Needs an erhai kernel with the iris decode-order output property (the 7.2
 line from +b10 on, or any 7.3-rc* line) for Firefox/Brave; the full erhai
 image ships it. Installing it pulls in libva-av1fix, the one library the AV1
 path needs on the client side (it carries the per-picture refresh set to the
 driver); ffmpeg-av1fix is only a Recommends because it fixes the same thing in
 the players (mpv, VLC), while the browsers bring their own code. The kernel
 and the browsers are the image's job, so nothing else is dragged in. After
 installing on a running desktop, log out and back in once.
 .
 The iris-vaapi source tree ships in /usr/src/oneplus-erhai-video/iris-vaapi.
 .
 1.3 (2026-10-23): the 1.2 payload ALSO shipped the vendored iris-vaapi's .git
 directory -- 44 entries: the object store, the hooks and commit signatures with the
 author's identity -- because that build still ran from a tree carrying the clone
 metadata.  No code changed (the driver is rebuilt from the same source, byte for
 byte); the tree is clean and build-video-deb.sh now REFUSES to pack a nested .git.
 1.5 (2026-11-17): AV1 hardware decoding.  The driver builds the AV1 frame
 headers itself (the V4L2 firmware wants them as raw bitstream, the VA-API
 client gets them as structures) and it kept getting three things wrong on real
 streams: the tile offsets had to be absolute inside the temporal unit, the
 sequence header was re-emitted in the middle of playback whenever a later
 frame flipped a superres/restoration/delta-q flag, and the DPB was rebuilt
 from a reference map it could not fully recover.  The driver now keeps its own
 DPB, honours the refresh set the client passes (libva, ffmpeg and chromium were
 extended to carry it), detects seeks by order hint and survives a lost
 reference, and refuses 10-bit AV1 instead of decoding it as 8-bit.  Verified on
 1080p and 4K AV1: Chromium/Brave (V4L2), mpv and VLC 4 (vaapi) decode on the
 VPU with no software fallback.  It also implements the legacy
 vaAcquireBufferHandle/vaReleaseBufferHandle pair, which VLC 3.x probes before
 it will display a VA-API surface at all.
 1.7 (2026-11-17): the driver lives in /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/dri only.
 1.5/1.6 also shipped a copy under /usr/local/lib/<multiarch>/dri because the
 patched libva was built with that driverdir; libva-av1fix now uses the distro
 directory, so the second copy (and the Conflicts on the old test package) is
 gone and a plain apt upgrade is enough.
 1.8 (2026-11-17): the driver source is the tree that was measured on the pad,
 not the later variant that ran AV1 one frame behind and derived the sequence
 header per picture.  With 1.7 the browsers decoded AV1 on the VPU but presented
 black frames: a Brave report showed VaapiVideoDecoder at ~52 pictures/s while
 the compositor received empty pictures.  Same source as the /usr/local build of
 2026-11-15 17:48 that renders correctly.
 1.9 (2026-11-17): hardware decode no longer needs environment variables.  A
 browser started from a desktop session that predates the variables ran without
 LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME and IRIS_VAAPI_AV1, so libva looked for its built-in default
 driver (msm_drv_video.so), found nothing and the browser fell back to dav1d --
 software AV1, no VPU.  The package now also installs the driver under that
 default name (a symlink to iris_drv_video.so) and the driver advertises AV1
 unless IRIS_VAAPI_AV1=0, which stays as the kill switch.  Hosts that still
 export the variables keep working unchanged.
 1.11 (2026-11-18): the Firefox AV1 kill switch ships.  Since 1.9 the driver
 advertises AV1 to every client, which includes Firefox -- and Firefox 160
 mishandles the erhai VPU (its stall-recovery loop ends in
 NS_ERROR_DOM_MEDIA_FATAL_ERR, and the firmware session churn once wedged the
 decoder hard enough to reboot the tablet).  The workaround existed only as a
 hand-written /usr/local/bin/firefox on the development pad, so every
 installed system still exposed AV1 to Firefox.  The package now ships that
 wrapper (PATH-shadowing, IRIS_VAAPI_AV1=0, exec of the real binary): Firefox
 keeps H.264/HEVC/VP9 on the chip and decodes AV1 with dav1d until the Firefox
 side is fixed.  Also: 10-bit (P010) AV1 decode, the loop-restoration type
 mismatch that made LR-using films fail to decode, and the per-client
 restoration type conventions (libavcodec raw syntax vs the DXVA order stock
 libva clients use) are in the 1.10 driver already.
 1.13 (2026-11-19): two decode-path fixes found while chasing a 4K AV1 picture
 freeze at a VP9->AV1 codec switch.  VA surface ids are now process-wide instead
 of per-VADisplay: a second VAContext in the same process (Chrome starts one
 VADisplay per decoder) restarted the id sequence at 1, so the client's
 id-keyed surface cache handed the compositor stale images of the first pool
 while the decoder wrote into new buffers it had never imported -- the visible
 result was a picture that stopped following the stream.  And a VP9 barrier
 timeout no longer fails vaEndPicture: the driver tries the firmware stall
 recovery, then serves the target's previous contents for that one frame.
 Chrome treats the error as a decode failure, destroys the decoder and
 re-creates it, which is what wedged presentation at the switch ("vaEndPicture
 failed, VA error: internal decoding error" immediately before the player was
 recreated).  Both were verified on the pad with 0 failures over a long 4K AV1
 run.  The freeze itself was Mutter withholding Wayland frame callbacks
 (oneplus-erhai-mutter +erhai3), not the driver.
 1.14 (2026-11-19): a seek restarts the firmware session, but the driver kept
 its cached copy of the encoder's AV1 sequence header, so the first key frame
 after the restart was submitted with the sequence header stripped; a fresh
 firmware session has none, answered VA_STATUS_ERROR_DECODING_ERROR, Chrome
 destroyed the decoder and the player sat on its loading animation.  The cache
 is now cleared on every session reset.  AV1 otherwise stays on the synthesized
 header path of 1.13: driving the firmware with the encoder's own access units
 (tested as IRIS_AV1_LIFT=1) is not reliable - the firmware answered
 "internal decoding error" at stream start on a 4K AV1 stream, Chrome fell back
 to other decoders and YouTube dropped the rendition to 480p.
